\title{FedXplain-IoMT: Diagnosing Cross-Protocol Explanation Divergence in Federated Intrusion Detection for Medical IoT}

\author{\uppercase{Swayam Swaroop Sahu}\authorrefmark{1}, 
\uppercase{Prakhar Sharma}\authorrefmark{1}, 
\uppercase{Geraldine Bessie Amali D}\authorrefmark{1}, 
and \uppercase{Kathiravan Srinivasan}\authorrefmark{1}, \IEEEmembership{Senior Member, IEEE}}

\address[1]{School of Computer Science and Engineering, Vellore Institute of Technology, Vellore 632014, India (e-mail: sahuswayamswaroop@gmail.com; prakhar.sharmaa2019@vitalum.ac.in; geraldine.amali@vit.ac.in; kathiravan.srinivasan@vit.ac.in)}

\markboth
{Sahu \headeretal: FedXplain-IoMT: Diagnosing Cross-Protocol Explanation Divergence}
{Sahu \headeretal: FedXplain-IoMT: Diagnosing Cross-Protocol Explanation Divergence}

\corresp{Corresponding author: Geraldine Bessie Amali D, Associate Professor Sr., School of Computer Science and Engineering, Vellore Institute of Technology, Vellore 632014, India (e-mail: geraldine.amali@vit.ac.in).}

\begin{abstract}
Healthcare privacy regulations such as HIPAA and GDPR strictly prohibit pooling raw clinical and device telemetry onto central servers, motivating the adoption of Federated Learning (FL) for collaborative Network Intrusion Detection Systems (NIDS). However, medical Internet of Things (IoMT) environments exhibit extreme physical and communication heterogeneity, concurrently deploying devices across disparate transport protocols including Wi-Fi, MQTT, and Bluetooth. Conventional FL benchmarks assess convergence solely through macro-averaged detection accuracy or F1 scores, masking catastrophic localized failure modes. In this paper, we present FedXplain-IoMT, a framework evaluating cross-protocol explanation divergence and model calibration on the realistic CICIoMT2024 benchmark across 40 physical IoMT devices and 19 distinct attack label values spanning DoS, DDoS, reconnaissance, and protocol-specific exploits. We demonstrate that standard Federated Averaging (FedAvg) induces a catastrophic collapse of benign traffic recall on the Bluetooth client down to 0.3666 (seed 42) and 0.4238 (seed 7), averaging 0.3952 despite a seemingly viable global macro F1 of 0.7988. Sweeping the proximal penalty $\mu \in \{0.001, 0.01, 0.05, 0.1\}$ in FedProx monotonically worsens this collapse, reducing Bluetooth benign recall to 0.0073--0.0343 at $\mu=0.1$ as proximal anchoring binds minority clients to majority attack gradients. Using SHAP (DeepExplainer) feature attributions, we diagnose this collapse as an output calibration failure rather than representational divergence: cross-client mean-$|\text{SHAP}|$ attribution rankings remain highly aligned ($\rho \approx 0.98$ on Bluetooth between FedPer and FedAvg). Decoupling the model via personalized federated learning (FedPer) preserves the shared trunk while personalizing the head, recovering 72.7\%--77.9\% of the lost Bluetooth benign-recall gap (reaching 0.8258 / 0.8721) and achieving 0.9241 average macro F1 with consistent decision logic ($\rho \ge 0.88$). Protocol-aware Clustered FL further demonstrates that segregating conflicting traffic distributions attains 0.9665 macro F1 and 0.9927 benign recall, proving that local calibration is indispensable for trustworthy medical intrusion detection.
\end{abstract}

\begin{keywords}
CICIoMT2024, explainable AI, federated learning, Internet of Medical Things, intrusion detection, personalized federated learning, SHAP.
\end{keywords}

\titlepgskip=-15pt

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}
\PARstart{T}{he} swift uptake of the Internet of Medical Things (IoMT) has brought about a fundamental change in patient care, with capabilities for continuous physiological telemetry, real-time remote diagnostics and the use of smart infusion pumps for automated drug delivery~\cite{nguyen2021federated}. Yet the ubiquity of network connectivity in clinical settings has also broadened their attack surface. Patient safety is put at risk by malicious intrusions on hospital equipment, be it a denial-of-service (DoS) assault on telemetry brokers or the unauthorised tampering of infusion parameters~\cite{dadkhah2024towards}.

Machine learning Network Intrusion Detection Systems (NIDS) are being put to work in greater numbers to spot irregularities in network flows and protect clinical infrastructure. For a centralized intrusion detection model to be of any use, however, it must be trained on raw packet captures and telemetry logs drawn from a variety of hospital wards, clinics and healthcare systems. In the case of healthcare networks, that kind of data pooling is heavily restricted under regulations like Europe's General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR)~\cite{nguyen2021federated} and the U.S. Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA)~\cite{hipaa2013security}. Federated Learning (FL)~\cite{mcmahan2017communication} is now the preferred way of circumventing this problem while maintaining privacy. Under FL, institutional clients can train a common global model in a distributed fashion; they do so by passing along parameter updates or gradients and leaving the actual network flow data where it belongs on the institution's own servers.

\subsection{The Hidden Pitfall: Cross-Protocol Heterogeneity}
Despite the promise of federated learning, hospitals are not homogeneous. All of these facilities share devices which use highly divergent physical and transport protocols, such as USB, MAC, and WLAN:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Wi-Fi (IEEE 802.11)}: High-speed wireless communications for mobile workstations, diagnostic imaging carts and central telemetry monitors.
    \item \textbf{MQTT (Message Queuing Telemetry Transport)}: Simple publish-subscribe messaging for monitors at the bedside, nurse call stations, or IoT sensor gateways.
    \item \textbf{Bluetooth / BLE (IEEE 802.15.1)}: Low-power short-range communication used by wearable glucose monitors, pulse oximeters, and handheld diagnostic wands.
\end{enumerate}

While these protocol silos are not connected to one another, they are participating in a federated learning federation. Evaluation tools have a dangerous blind spot: they measure model performance by its overall classifiability, or F1 scores for all of the clients.

As we show in the results of this study, global metrics can mask catastrophic local failures. For example, in the standard case of Federated Averaging (FedAvg)~\cite{mcmahan2017communication}, our average macro F1 is 0.7988. But, this global total masks a severe failure on the minority Bluetooth client, whose benign traffic recall drops to 0.3666 for seed 42 and 0.4238 for seed 7. Between 57\% and 63\% of the legitimate medical Bluetooth packets are falsely reported as malicious. Such a high false alarm rate causes alert fatigue and automated network containment systems to disconnect the life-critical sensors.

Worse, applying FedProx~\cite{li2020federated} (a standard algorithm for counteracting client drift with non-IID data) does not resolve the collapse but makes it worse. Given a proximal sweep $\mu \in \{0.001, 0.01, 0.05, 0.1\}$, Bluetooth benign recall drops to 0.0073--0.0343 at $\mu=0.1$. Proximal regularization ties the local Bluetooth client to the global model which is overly biased towards the majority Wi-Fi client (96.4\% of attack flows).

\subsection{Explainability as a First-Class Diagnostic Axis}
Why does standard federated aggregation fail so severely on minority protocols? Traditional machine learning metrics cannot answer whether the global model fails because:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item It learns divergent, conflicting feature representations across protocols; or
    \item It learns a sound shared feature representation, but shifts its final classification threshold due to class imbalance tension.
\end{enumerate}
To date, no federated intrusion detection benchmark evaluates \emph{cross-client explanation divergence} as a primary evaluation metric. 

In this work, we introduce \textbf{FedXplain-IoMT}, an empirical framework combining post-hoc feature attribution using SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations)~\cite{lundberg2017unified} with multi-dimensional divergence metrics (Top-5 Jaccard similarity, Spearman rank correlation $\rho$, and Cosine similarity). By computing attributions on held-out attack samples, we show that feature attribution rankings between the personalized model and the collapsed FedAvg model remain strongly correlated ($\rho = 0.9888$ on Bluetooth across both seeds). This demonstrates that the failure is not a breakdown of internal feature representations, but rather an \textbf{output calibration failure} driven by shared final layer weights.

Guided by this diagnostic finding, we deploy Personalized Federated Learning via FedPer~\cite{arivazhagan2019federated}, which shares the feature extraction trunk while decoupling the classification head. FedPer recovers 72.7\%--77.9\% of the lost Bluetooth benign-recall gap (reaching 0.8258 / 0.8721) and delivers an average macro F1 of 0.9241, while maintaining high cross-client explanation consistency ($\rho \ge 0.88$). Furthermore, we evaluate protocol-aware Clustered Federated Learning (Cluster-FL)~\cite{sattler2020clustered}, which clusters clients into protocol-compatible groups ({Wi-Fi: 0}, {MQTT, Bluetooth: 1}) to achieve an average macro F1 of 0.9665 and a Bluetooth benign recall of 0.9927.

\subsection{Contributions}
The primary contributions of this paper are:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Cross-Protocol Explanation Divergence Benchmark}: We establish the first systematic federated learning benchmark evaluating cross-client explanation divergence across heterogeneous physical protocols (Wi-Fi, MQTT, Bluetooth) on the CICIoMT2024 dataset~\cite{dadkhah2024towards}.
    \item \textbf{Empirical Diagnosis of Calibration Failure}: We reveal that standard FedAvg induces a severe benign recall collapse on minority protocols (0.3952 mean recall) and that FedProx monotonically deepens this failure (down to 0.0073 recall). Using SHAP DeepExplainer, we prove that this failure is an output calibration and threshold shift rather than internal representation divergence ($\rho \approx 0.98$).
    \item \textbf{Personalized \& Clustered Recovery}: We show that FedPer's personalized classification head restores 72.7\%--77.9\% of the benign recall gap while preserving shared representation logic, and Clustered FL isolates protocol interference to reach 0.9665 macro F1 and 0.9927 benign recall.
    \item \textbf{Zero-Day \& Data-Scarcity Generalization}: We prove that while local models appear strong on in-distribution data, they suffer acute vulnerability to unseen cross-protocol intrusions ($<3.0\%$ recall on novel floods), whereas FedPer detects $97.3\%$--$99.9\%$ of zero-day attacks and improves sample-starved convergence by $+5.4\%$ macro F1.
    \item \textbf{Reproducible Open-Source Framework}: We release the complete codebase, offline interactive dashboard, strict 45-feature schema assertions, and verification gate scripts under an open-source license.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Paper Organization}
The remainder of this article is structured as follows: Section~\ref{sec:related_work} reviews related literature in federated NIDS, explainable AI, and IoMT datasets. Section~\ref{sec:methods} presents the end-to-end system architecture, system and threat models, dataset partitioning, neural network architecture, federated optimization algorithms, and the explanation divergence framework. Section~\ref{sec:results} presents comprehensive empirical results, figures, and tabular comparisons. Section~\ref{sec:discussion} discusses clinical implications, threats to validity, and limitations. Finally, Section~\ref{sec:conclusion} concludes the paper.

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\subsection{Federated Learning for Network Intrusion Detection}
Federated learning has gained widespread attention in cybersecurity for training intrusion detection models across distributed corporate and medical enterprise networks without sharing raw packet data~\cite{nguyen2021federated}. McMahan \emph{et al.}~\cite{mcmahan2017communication} introduced Federated Averaging (FedAvg), which aggregates local client weights proportional to dataset size. However, FedAvg struggles under non-IID (non-identically and independently distributed) client data.

To mitigate client drift under statistical heterogeneity, Li \emph{et al.}~\cite{li2020federated} introduced FedProx, which adds a proximal regularization term $\frac{\mu}{2}\|w - w_t\|^2$ to each client's local loss function. FedProx bounds the distance between local parameters and the global model. While effective in standard synthetic benchmarks, we demonstrate that when data heterogeneity arises from conflicting physical communication protocols and severe class imbalance, FedProx's proximal anchoring causes minority clients to be overwhelmed by majority protocol gradients.

Personalized federated learning techniques relax the requirement of learning a single global model. Arivazhagan \emph{et al.}~\cite{arivazhagan2019federated} proposed FedPer, which divides neural networks into base layers (shared representation trunk) and personalization layers (private head). By sharing common flow representation while permitting each client to learn a specialized classification boundary, FedPer provides an effective architecture for heterogeneous environments. Sattler \emph{et al.}~\cite{sattler2020clustered} proposed Clustered Federated Learning (Cluster-FL), which partitions clients into distinct clusters based on cosine similarity of gradient updates, eliminating inter-cluster gradient conflict.

\subsection{Explainable AI (XAI) in Cybersecurity}
Machine learning models deployed for network security operate as black boxes, making it difficult for security operations center (SOC) analysts and clinical engineers to validate why specific traffic flows are classified as attacks~\cite{guo2023explainable}. Post-hoc explainability methods, particularly SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations)~\cite{lundberg2017unified}, assign game-theoretic credit to input features for each prediction.

In centralized network traffic classification, SHAP has been utilized to identify leading attack indicators, such as packet length variance, TCP window sizes, and inter-arrival times. However, prior studies investigate SHAP explanations exclusively in centralized configurations or on homogeneous synthetic federations. None examine whether federated model convergence produces consistent or divergent decision logic across physically heterogeneous protocol silos.

\subsection{IoMT and IoT Security Benchmarks}
Early NIDS research relied on legacy benchmarks including KDD Cup 99 and NSL-KDD~\cite{tavallaee2009detailed}. While historically foundational, these datasets lack modern network protocol dynamics, IoT traffic behaviors, and contemporary attack vectors. Subsequent benchmarks such as UNSW-NB15~\cite{moustafa2015unsw}, BoT-IoT~\cite{koroniotis2019towards}, and CICIoT2023~\cite{neto2023ciciot2023} introduced realistic IoT and botnet traffic.

However, these datasets do not capture the unique multi-protocol environment of clinical healthcare. To address this deficiency, Dadkhah \emph{et al.}~\cite{dadkhah2024towards} developed the \textbf{CICIoMT2024} dataset at the Canadian Institute for Cybersecurity (CIC). CICIoMT2024 is the first multidimensional dataset captured from 40 real physical IoMT devices across three physical communication protocols: Wi-Fi, MQTT, and Bluetooth.

\subsection{Research Gap}
Existing research has focused almost exclusively on optimizing global detection metrics (accuracy, precision, recall, F1) across aggregated datasets. Crucially, the literature has overlooked the phenomenon of \textbf{cross-client explanation divergence} in multi-protocol federations, as well as the risk that global parameter sharing can inadvertently suppress benign traffic recognition in minority protocols. FedXplain-IoMT directly bridges this gap.

\section{System Architecture and Methodology}
\label{sec:methods}

\subsection{End-to-End System Architecture}
\label{subsec:architecture}
The overall architectural workflow of the proposed FedXplain-IoMT framework is illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:system_architecture}. The framework operates as an end-to-end, privacy-preserving research and deployment pipeline structured into five interconnected modular subsystems:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Heterogeneous Telemetry Ingestion and Clinical Protocol Partitioning}: Real-world network telemetry is ingested from $40$ physical IoMT devices distributed across three physically heterogeneous communication layers: clinical Wi-Fi (IEEE 802.11 monitor mode), bedside MQTT message brokers (TCP port 1883), and wearable Bluetooth/BLE peripherals (HCI H4 capture). Each client operates within an isolated local subnet governed by strict privacy boundaries (HIPAA/GDPR), retaining its raw telemetry data locally.
    \item \textbf{Feature Engineering and 45-Feature Schema Guardrails}: Raw flows undergo rigorous sanitization. Non-generalizable identification artifacts (IP addresses, MAC addresses, TCP/UDP port numbers, and timestamps) are removed to prevent trivial shortcut learning. An invariant 45-feature schema assertion is enforced across all client data partitions to guarantee identical dimensionality ($x \in \mathbb{R}^{45}$). To eliminate data leakage, protocol-specific standard scalers ($\text{StandardScaler}$) are fit strictly on the stratified 80\% training partitions and applied to the 20\% test sets.
    \item \textbf{Multi-Algorithm Federated Optimization Engine}: Distributed model training is orchestrated across clients using the Flower framework with PyTorch. The engine supports six distinct learning paradigms: Centralized training (theoretical upper-bound ceiling), Local-Only training (isolated per-client control), standard Federated Averaging (FedAvg), Proximal-regularized FL (FedProx with $\mu \in \{0.001, 0.01, 0.05, 0.1\}$), Personalized Federated Learning (FedPer, decoupling a shared 5,024-parameter trunk from a 33-parameter local head), and Clustered Federated Learning (Cluster-FL, protocol-aligned clustering).
    \item \textbf{Model-Agnostic Explainable AI (XAI) Attribution Pipeline}: Following federated convergence, the framework extracts game-theoretic feature attributions using SHAP DeepExplainer. For each client, attributions are computed on 200 held-out attack samples referenced against a protocol-specific background of 100 benign samples, generating empirical feature importance distributions $\bar{\phi}_{k,j}$ without accessing other clients' private data.
    \item \textbf{Diagnostic Explanation Divergence and Defense Evaluation}: Cross-protocol attribution consistency is audited across clients using Spearman rank correlation ($\rho$), Top-5 Jaccard similarity, and Cosine vector similarity against an empirical inter-seed noise floor $\Delta_{\text{noise}}$. This diagnostic pipeline isolates the exact mechanism of model failures (representation collapse vs. decision boundary calibration shift). The framework further evaluates defense resilience under unobserved cross-protocol zero-day attacks and extreme clinical data scarcity ($N=50$ samples).
\end{enumerate}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.92\textwidth]{system_architecture.png}
\caption{End-to-end FedXplain-IoMT system architecture: (1) Heterogeneous IoMT protocol partitions capturing real clinical traffic from 40 physical devices across Wi-Fi, MQTT, and Bluetooth; (2) Data Preprocessing, Protocol-Specific Scaling, and Strict 45-Feature Schema Enforcement; (3) Multi-Algorithm Federated Learning Engine (coordinating Centralized ceiling, Local-Only control, FedAvg, FedProx, FedPer, and Cluster-FL); (4) Model-Agnostic Explainable AI Pipeline utilizing SHAP DeepExplainer to compute feature attributions $\bar{\phi}_{k,j}$ on held-out test attacks; (5) Diagnostic Explanation Divergence Analyzer and Personalized Defenses diagnosing output calibration failures ($\rho = 0.9888$) and providing cross-protocol zero-day generalization.}
\label{fig:system_architecture}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{System Model and Threat Model}
We model a distributed hospital network consisting of $K=3$ institutional protocol partitions: Wi-Fi, MQTT, and Bluetooth. Each partition represents an isolated clinical subnet:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Client 1 ($\mathcal{D}_1$): High-throughput clinical Wi-Fi subnet.
    \item Client 2 ($\mathcal{D}_2$): Bedside MQTT telemetry message broker.
    \item Client 3 ($\mathcal{D}_3$): Wearable Bluetooth diagnostic peripherals.
\end{itemize}

We assume an \textbf{honest-but-curious} central aggregation server. Participating hospital clients execute the federated optimization protocol faithfully, but the server and clients are untrusted with raw patient or device network traffic. Regulatory mandates (HIPAA/GDPR) strictly prohibit exchanging raw flow features $\mathcal{D}_k = \{(x_i, y_i)\}$ between clients or with the server.

The threat model incorporates 19 distinct attack label values spanning Denial of Service (DoS), Distributed Denial of Service (DDoS), Reconnaissance scanning, Address Resolution Protocol (ARP) spoofing, and protocol-specific exploits (MQTT flood/malformed-data attacks and a Bluetooth DoS class) executed against active IoMT devices~\cite{dadkhah2024towards}. The classification objective is binary intrusion detection: distinguishing benign traffic ($y=0$) from malicious attack traffic ($y=1$).

\subsection{Dataset Partitions and Preprocessing}
We utilize the CICIoMT2024 benchmark~\cite{dadkhah2024towards}. The dataset comprises 166,336 total network flow records across the three protocols. The dataset distribution is summarized in Table~\ref{tab:dataset_stats}.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\caption{CICIoMT2024 Protocol Partition Statistics}
\label{tab:dataset_stats}
\centering
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{|l|c|c|c|c|c|}
\hline
\textbf{Protocol} & \textbf{Total Flows} & \textbf{Train (80\%)} & \textbf{Test (20\%)} & \textbf{Attack \%} & \textbf{Benign \%} \\
\hline
Wi-Fi & 99,993 & 79,994 & 19,999 & 96.4\% & 3.6\% \\
MQTT & 49,997 & 39,997 & 10,000 & 63.2\% & 36.8\% \\
Bluetooth & 16,346 & 13,076 & 3,270 & 41.2\% & 58.8\% \\
\hline
\textbf{Total} & \textbf{166,336} & \textbf{133,067} & \textbf{33,269} & --- & --- \\
\hline
\end{tabular}%
}
\end{table}

The physical capture methodology introduces intrinsic statistical and protocol heterogeneity:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Wi-Fi}: Captured via 802.11 monitor mode; dominated by high-volume flood and reconnaissance attacks (96.4\% attack flows).
    \item \textbf{MQTT}: Captured from message brokers handling TCP port 1883 telemetry (63.2\% attack flows).
    \item \textbf{Bluetooth}: Captured from Host Controller Interface (HCI) H4 packet captures ($\text{.pcap}$). Because Bluetooth frames do not natively use TCP/IP headers, non-applicable TCP/IP flag fields are set to zero in the flow extractor (58.8\% benign flows).
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Feature Guardrails and Normalization}
Every data partition is strictly asserted to contain exactly 45 network flow features. Non-generalizable identifier columns (source/destination IP addresses, MAC addresses, port numbers, and timestamps) are removed to prevent models from learning trivial identity shortcuts. 

Data is partitioned using an 80/20 train/test split with label stratification ($\text{stratify}=y$). To prevent data snooping, a standard z-score scaler ($\text{StandardScaler}$) is fitted strictly on the training partition ($X_{\text{train}}$) and applied via transformation to the test partition ($X_{\text{test}}$).

\subsection{Neural Network Architecture: IoMTMLP}
We formulate a lightweight multi-layer perceptron, designated \textbf{IoMTMLP} and implemented in PyTorch~\cite{paszke2019pytorch}, tailored for low-latency inference on resource-constrained IoMT gateway devices. The model architecture is defined as:
\begin{align}
    h_1 &= \text{ReLU}(W_1 x + b_1) \\
    \tilde{h}_1 &= \text{Dropout}(h_1, p=0.2) \\
    h_2 &= \text{ReLU}(W_2 \tilde{h}_1 + b_2) \\
    \hat{y} &= \sigma(W_3 h_2 + b_3)
\end{align}
where $x \in \mathbb{R}^{45}$, $W_1 \in \mathbb{R}^{64 \times 45}$, $b_1 \in \mathbb{R}^{64}$, $W_2 \in \mathbb{R}^{32 \times 64}$, $b_2 \in \mathbb{R}^{32}$, $W_3 \in \mathbb{R}^{1 \times 32}$, and $b_3 \in \mathbb{R}^1$. The activation $\sigma(z) = (1 + e^{-z})^{-1}$ is the standard logistic sigmoid function. 

The model contains exactly 6 parameter arrays comprising 5,057 total trainable parameters. In Personalized Federated Learning (FedPer), the network is partitioned as follows:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Shared Representation Trunk (Layers 1--2)}: Parameters $\{W_1, b_1, W_2, b_2\}$ comprising 5,024 weights and biases (99.35\% of total), which are communicated and aggregated across clients.
    \item \textbf{Personalized Head (Layer 3)}: Parameters $\{W_3, b_3\}$ comprising 33 weights and biases (0.65\% of total), which are trained and preserved locally on each client.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Federated Optimization Algorithms}
We benchmark five distinct learning paradigms:

\subsubsection{Centralized Benchmark (Upper Bound)}
Data from all three clients are pooled into a single centralized repository and trained for 10 epochs using mini-batch Adam optimization ($\eta = 10^{-3}$, batch size 64). This represents the empirical performance ceiling in the absence of privacy restrictions.

\subsubsection{Local-Only Baseline (Control)}
Each client trains an isolated IoMTMLP solely on its local training partition for 30 epochs without any parameter sharing. This isolates the baseline performance of localized models and answers whether federating helps or harms individual clients.

\subsubsection{Federated Averaging (FedAvg)}
In each communication round $t \in \{1, \dots, T\}$, the central server broadcasts global parameters $w_t$. Each client $k \in \{1, \dots, K\}$ initializes with $w_t$ and performs $E=3$ local epochs of stochastic gradient descent minimizing binary cross-entropy loss:
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{L}_k(w) = -\frac{1}{n_k} \sum_{i=1}^{n_k} \left[ y_i \log \hat{y}_i + (1 - y_i) \log (1 - \hat{y}_i) \right]
\end{equation}
The server aggregates local updates weighted by client sample volume:
\begin{equation}
    w_{t+1} = \sum_{k=1}^K \frac{n_k}{n} w_{t+1}^k
\end{equation}
where $n_k$ is the training size of client $k$, and $n = \sum_k n_k = 133,067$.

\subsubsection{Federated Proximal (FedProx)}
To constrain local client drift, FedProx introduces a proximal regularization term to the local objective~\cite{li2020federated}:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:fedprox}
    \min_{w} h_k(w; w_t) = \mathcal{L}_k(w) + \frac{\mu}{2} \|w - w_t\|_2^2
\end{equation}
where $\mu \ge 0$ controls the penalty strength, and $w_t$ is the detached global model weight vector broadcast at the start of round $t$. We evaluate a comprehensive logarithmic sweep $\mu \in \{0.001, 0.01, 0.05, 0.1\}$.

\subsubsection{Personalized Federated Learning (FedPer)}
Clients share trunk weights $w_{\text{trunk}} = \{W_1, b_1, W_2, b_2\}$ through standard FedAvg aggregation, while head parameters $w_{\text{head}}^k = \{W_3^k, b_3^k\}$ are maintained strictly locally on client $k$. During local training, gradients backpropagate through both head and trunk, but only trunk updates are transmitted to the server.

\subsubsection{Clustered Federated Learning (Cluster-FL)}
Clients are partitioned into protocol-compatible clusters: $\mathcal{C}_0 = \{\text{Wi-Fi}\}$ and $\mathcal{C}_1 = \{\text{MQTT}, \text{Bluetooth}\}$. Federated averaging is executed independently within each cluster, completely preventing cross-cluster gradient interference.

\subsubsection{Hyperparameter Standardization}
All federated algorithms are executed over $T=10$ communication rounds with $E=3$ local epochs per round (yielding 30 total local epochs, matching the Local-only control's compute), orchestrated with the Flower federated learning framework~\cite{beutel2020flower}. Mini-batch size is set to 64, optimized using Adam with a learning rate $\eta = 10^{-3}$. All experiments are evaluated across two fixed random seeds: $\{42, 7\}$.

\subsection{Explanation Divergence Framework}
To quantify explanation consistency, we employ SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations) via the SHAP DeepExplainer framework~\cite{lundberg2017unified}. For each client, post-hoc attributions are computed on 200 held-out attack samples from the test partition, referenced against a background dataset of 100 protocol-specific benign samples.

For each client $k$ and feature $j \in \{1, \dots, 45\}$, we compute the mean absolute SHAP value:
\begin{equation}
    \bar{\phi}_{k, j} = \frac{1}{N_{\text{expl}}} \sum_{i=1}^{N_{\text{expl}}} |\phi_{k, j}^{(i)}|
\end{equation}
yielding a 45-dimensional global feature importance vector $\bar{\phi}_k \in \mathbb{R}^{45}$.

We define three complementary pairwise divergence metrics between client $A$ and client $B$:

\subsubsection{Top-5 Jaccard Similarity}
Let $\mathcal{T}_5(A)$ denote the set of the top-5 most important features for client $A$ according to $\bar{\phi}_A$. The Top-5 Jaccard similarity measures subset overlap:
\begin{equation}
    J(A, B) = \frac{|\mathcal{T}_5(A) \cap \mathcal{T}_5(B)|}{|\mathcal{T}_5(A) \cup \mathcal{T}_5(B)|}
\end{equation}
where $J \in [0, 1]$. $J=1$ denotes identical top-5 feature sets, while $J=0$ indicates completely disjoint top-tier feature selections.

\subsubsection{Spearman Rank Correlation ($\rho$)}
To assess monotonic ranking agreement across the entire 45-dimensional feature space, we compute the Spearman rank correlation coefficient:
\begin{equation}
    \rho(\bar{\phi}_A, \bar{\phi}_B) = 1 - \frac{6 \sum_{j=1}^{d} d_j^2}{d(d^2 - 1)}
\end{equation}
where $d=45$ is the feature dimensionality, and $d_j = \text{rank}(\bar{\phi}_{A, j}) - \text{rank}(\bar{\phi}_{B, j})$ is the difference between the ranks of feature $j$.

\subsubsection{Cosine Similarity}
To measure directional vector alignment in feature attribution space:
\begin{equation}
    \cos(\bar{\phi}_A, \bar{\phi}_B) = \frac{\bar{\phi}_A \cdot \bar{\phi}_B}{\|\bar{\phi}_A\|_2 \|\bar{\phi}_B\|_2}
\end{equation}

\subsubsection{Seed Spread and Noise-Floor Calibration}
With $n=2$ random seeds ($\{42, 7\}$), statistical variance cannot be captured via standard confidence intervals. We establish an empirical \textbf{noise floor} defined as the absolute inter-seed spread:
\begin{equation}
    \Delta_{\text{noise}} = |M_{\text{seed42}} - M_{\text{seed7}}|
\end{equation}
Any difference between federated aggregation strategies that falls below this noise floor is reported as within-variance. This methodology provides a grounded, honest evaluation baseline for directional claims.

\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig1_detection_macro_f1.png}
\caption{Per-client and average macro F1 scores across evaluated learning paradigms. While centralized, local-only, cluster-FL, and FedPer maintain robust detection across all clients, standard FedAvg and FedProx suffer severe performance degradation driven by the Bluetooth client collapse.}
\label{fig:detection_macro_f1}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.88\linewidth]{fig2_spearman_divergence_heatmap.png}
\caption{Cross-client explanation divergence heatmap displaying Spearman rank correlation ($\rho$) across protocol pairs for each federated method (averaged over seeds). The seed-to-seed noise floor is explicitly annotated, demonstrating that Wi-Fi and MQTT retain strong attribution alignment under shared-model federation ($\rho > 0.73$ for FedAvg, FedProx, and FedPer), while Bluetooth exhibits intrinsic physical protocol divergence.}
\label{fig:spearman_divergence_heatmap}
\end{figure*}

