Under standard FedAvg, the global model achieves an average macro F1 of 0.7988 (0.7966 for seed 42, 0.8011 for seed 7). However, inspection of per-client metrics exposes an acute failure mode:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Wi-Fi}: Reaches 0.9375 / 0.9332 macro F1 with 0.8893 / 0.8797 benign recall.
    \item \textbf{MQTT}: Reaches 0.8409 / 0.8218 macro F1 with 0.6463 / 0.5984 benign recall.
    \item \textbf{Bluetooth}: Suffers a catastrophic collapse, achieving only 0.6112 / 0.6483 macro F1 and a benign recall of \textbf{0.3666} (seed 42) and \textbf{0.4238} (seed 7).
\end{itemize}
In stark contrast, the \textbf{Local-only} baseline achieves an average macro F1 of 0.9708 (Wi-Fi: 0.9406, MQTT: 0.9828, Bluetooth: 0.9891) and maintains near-perfect Bluetooth benign recall of \textbf{0.9984} (seed 42) and \textbf{0.9990} (seed 7). Standard federated averaging actively destroys local benign classification on the minority protocol.

\subsection{Monotonic Failure of FedProx Regularization}
A standard assumption in federated optimization is that proximal regularization counteracts non-IID client drift. We evaluate FedProx across $\mu \in \{0.001, 0.01, 0.05, 0.1\}$.

As documented in Table~\ref{tab:detection_results} and illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:bluetooth_mu_sweep}, FedProx does not rescue the minority protocol; rather, it \textbf{monotonically worsens the collapse}:
\begin{itemize}
    \item At $\mu = 0.001$: Bluetooth benign recall drops to 0.2090 (seed 42) and 0.1092 (seed 7), averaging 0.1591.
    \item At $\mu = 0.01$: Bluetooth benign recall falls to 0.0364 (seed 42) and 0.0333 (seed 7), averaging 0.0349.
    \item At $\mu = 0.05$: Bluetooth benign recall drops to 0.0192 (seed 42) and 0.0322 (seed 7), averaging 0.0257.
    \item At $\mu = 0.1$: Bluetooth benign recall plummets to \textbf{0.0073} (seed 42) and \textbf{0.0343} (seed 7), averaging 0.0208.
\end{itemize}
At $\mu = 0.1$, the model falsely classifies between 96.6\% and 99.3\% of legitimate medical Bluetooth packets as cyberattacks.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig3_bluetooth_mu_sweep.png}
\caption{Bluetooth benign recall as a function of the FedProx proximal penalty $\mu$. Proximal regularization exhibits a strictly monotonic collapse, driving benign recall from 0.3952 under FedAvg down to 0.0208 at $\mu=0.1$ due to majority-client gradient anchoring.}
\label{fig:bluetooth_mu_sweep}
\end{figure}

The mechanism driving this failure is sample-weighted gradient dominance. The Wi-Fi client possesses 79,994 training samples with a 96.4\% attack prevalence, exerting overwhelming pull on the global model parameters $w_t$. When proximal regularization $\frac{\mu}{2}\|w - w_t\|^2$ is enforced on the Bluetooth client (which has only 13,076 samples and 58.8\% benign traffic), the local model is penalized for deviating from $w_t$. Increasing $\mu$ strips the Bluetooth client of its capacity to preserve its benign decision boundary, forcing it to mimic the attack-biased global model.

\subsection{SHAP Diagnosis: Calibration vs. Representation}
To determine why the Bluetooth client fails, we analyze the post-hoc SHAP attributions generated by the SHAP DeepExplorer. We decide that the failure is caused by representational misalignment or a miscalibration in the decision boundary.

Table~\ref{tab:divergence_results} shows the pairwise explanation divergence of the protocols. 

