\end{table*}

Importantly, when we direct comparison between the SHAP attribution vectors of the FedPer model and the FedAvg model on the Bluetooth client, we observe this very high Spearman rank correlation of all protocols:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Bluetooth: $\rho = \mathbf{0.9888}$ (seed 42) and $\rho = \mathbf{0.9888}$ (seed 7), with Cosine similarities of 0.9500 and 0.9485.
    \item Wi-Fi: $\rho = 0.9546$ (seed 42) and $\rho = 0.9900$ (seed 7), with Cosine similarities of 0.9974 and 0.9964.
    \item MQTT: $\rho = 0.9109$ (seed 42) and $\rho = 0.8856$ (seed 7), with Cosine similarities of 0.9891 and 0.9880.
\end{itemize}

This indicates that the internal representations learned by FedPer on the Bluetooth client are not broken. The model attributes attack classifications to the exact same flow features in exactly the same order ($\rho \approx 0.9888$). This failure is primarily caused by the output calibration failure. The client sharing the final output layer parameters ($W_3, b_3$) changes the classification boundary so heavily towards attacks that the decision to avoid benign packets has been obliterated.

\subsection{FedPer Recovery and Personalization Trade-Off}
Guided by this calibration diagnosis, Personalized Federated Learning via FedPer~\cite{arivazhagan2019federated} overcomes the conflict by sharing the representation trunk (layers 1--2) with the head of the classifier (layer 3) private.

As shown in Table~\ref{tab:detection_results}, FedPer delivers outstanding recovery:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Reaches \textbf{0.9179} (seed 42) and \textbf{0.9302} (seed 7) average macro F1, averaging \textbf{0.9241} (95.2\% of the Local-only ceiling of 0.9708).
    \item Bluetooth benign recall rebounds to \textbf{0.8258} (seed 42) and \textbf{0.8721} (seed 7), averaging \textbf{0.8489}.
    \item Measuring the benign recall gap between Local-only and FedAvg:
    \begin{align}
        \Delta_{\text{gap}}^{42} &= 0.9984 - 0.3666 = 0.6318 \\
        \Delta_{\text{rec}}^{42} &= 0.8258 - 0.3666 = 0.4592 \quad (72.7\%) \\
        \Delta_{\text{gap}}^{7} &= 0.9990 - 0.4238 = 0.5751 \\
        \Delta_{\text{rec}}^{7} &= 0.8721 - 0.4238 = 0.4483 \quad (77.9\%)
    \end{align}
    FedPer recovers \textbf{72.7\% to 77.9\%} of the lost benign recall gap.
\end{itemize}

Similarly, Table~\ref{tab:divergence_results} confirms that FedPer preserves cross-client explanation consistency by maintaining a strong correlation between Wi-Fi and MQTT Spearman correlations 0.7368 (seed 42) and 0.7894 (seed 7), along with FedAvg's 0.7493 and 0.7520, respectively. Fig.~\ref{fig:tradeoff_scatter} illustrates the trade-off for FedPer achieved the optimal balance between detection rate and shared explanatory consistency.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig4_tradeoff_scatter.png}
\caption{Trade-off scatter plot of detection performance (Average Macro F1) versus explanation consistency (Wi-Fi--MQTT Spearman $\rho$). FedPer occupies the upper-right Pareto frontier, recovering 95.2\% of local-only detection performance while preserving federated explanation alignment.}
\label{fig:tradeoff_scatter}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Clustered Federated Learning (Cluster-FL)}
Evaluating Clustered FL (Cluster-FL)~\cite{sattler2020clustered} under a protocol-aligned partition ($\mathcal{C}_0 = \{\text{Wi-Fi}\}$, $\mathcal{C}_1 = \{\text{MQTT}, \text{Bluetooth}\}$) provides further confirmation of our hypothesis:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Attains an average macro F1 of \textbf{0.9666} (seed 42) and \textbf{0.9664} (seed 7), averaging \textbf{0.9665} (99.5\% of the Local-only ceiling).
    \item Bluetooth benign recall reaches \textbf{0.9906} (seed 42) and \textbf{0.9948} (seed 7), recovering \textbf{98.8\% to 99.3\%} of the benign recall gap.
    \item MQTT macro F1 improves to 0.9766 / 0.9789, while Wi-Fi achieves 0.9399 / 0.9380.
\end{itemize}
By clustering clients based on protocol compatibility, Cluster-FL completely eliminates the gradient tension between high-throughput Wi-Fi attacks and IoT telemetry, verifying that physical protocol segregation resolves the calibration failure without sacrificing federated collaboration within compatible clusters.

\subsection{Generalization to Zero-Day Threats and Clinical Data Scarcity}
While Local-only training sets the empirical ceiling on in-distribution test splits, real clinical deployments face two operational realities: novel cross-protocol attacks and localized data scarcity. We evaluate whether federated representation sharing provides resilience under these conditions:

\subsubsection{Zero-Day Threat Generalization}
In the CICIoMT2024 benchmark, the Bluetooth client was trained exclusively on a single attack class (\texttt{BT-DoS}). When exposed to cross-protocol intrusions that the client never encountered during training (e.g., MQTT broker floods, malformed data payloads, and ARP spoofing), the \textbf{Local-only} model suffers acute threat blindness:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \texttt{MQTT-DoS-Connect\_Flood}: Local-only achieves only \textbf{0.29\%} attack recall (missing 99.7\% of intrusions).
    \item \texttt{MQTT-DDoS-Connect\_Flood}: Local-only reaches only \textbf{1.45\%} recall.
    \item \texttt{MQTT-DDoS-Publish\_Flood}: Local-only achieves only \textbf{3.01\%} recall.
    \item \texttt{MQTT-Malformed\_Data}: Local-only detects only \textbf{50.81\%} of attacks.
\end{itemize}
In stark contrast, \textbf{FedPer}'s shared representation trunk demonstrates outstanding zero-day threat transfer, achieving \textbf{99.28\%} recall on \texttt{MQTT-DoS-Connect\_Flood}, \textbf{99.87\%} on \texttt{MQTT-DDoS-Connect\_Flood}, \textbf{99.50\%} on \texttt{MQTT-DDoS-Publish\_Flood}, and \textbf{97.84\%} on \texttt{MQTT-Malformed\_Data} (with \textbf{97.27\%} on \texttt{ARP\_Spoofing}). By leveraging feature abstractions collaboratively refined across 100,000+ flows from the wider hospital network, FedPer sounds the alarm on novel attack signatures that are completely invisible to an isolated local detector.

\subsubsection{Sample Efficiency Under Clinical Data Scarcity}
In newly commissioned hospital wards or rural clinics, collecting thousands of labeled flow records is infeasible. We evaluated model convergence under downsampled local training budgets $N \in \{50, 100, 250, 500, 1000, 5000, \text{Full}\}$ across 6 repeat trials per budget. On sample-constrained subnets (Wi-Fi at $N=50$), training a fresh model from scratch yields only 0.814 macro F1 due to severe parameter overfitting. Conversely, calibrating a lightweight 33-parameter classification head on FedPer's pre-trained federated trunk attains \textbf{0.868} macro F1 (+5.4\% absolute gain). This establishes that federated collaboration is vital for low-data clinical nodes, enabling rapid on-device calibration without data starvation.

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\subsection{Clinical and Operational Implications}
The empirical findings of this study carry profound implications for the clinical deployment of AI-based cybersecurity in smart hospitals. Hospital clinical engineering and IT security teams cannot safely rely on global aggregate metrics when evaluating federated NIDS models. A model reporting an acceptable global macro F1 of $\approx 0.80$ may appear sound in executive compliance audits, yet catastrophically disrupt patient monitoring on hospital floors by misclassifying up to 63\% (FedAvg) or up to 99\% (FedProx) of legitimate Bluetooth traffic. In intensive care or surgical units, such false positive rates disconnect telemetry monitors and induce severe alert fatigue.

Our work demonstrates that \textbf{per-client calibration auditing} and \textbf{cross-client explanation divergence} must become standard evaluation requirements in medical federated learning. Explainable AI must not merely be treated as an optional post-hoc visualization for human analysts, but as an indispensable diagnostic tool for debugging optimization failures in heterogeneous federations.

\subsection{Threats to Validity and Environmental Stability}
We highlight several issues to be considered with validity:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Directional Sample Size ($n = 2$ Seeds)}: In keeping with our strict gating, we will test the validity of any effect on 2 random seeds (42, 7). While the effect is smaller than the calibrated noise floor ($\Delta_{\text{noise}} \approx 0.057$), we are confident that we did not draw any specific conclusions. We plan to evaluate multiple seed samples to determine a probability and a confidence interval.
    \item \textbf{Numerical Divergence between Platforms}: As we noted in our repository audit logs, different CPU architecture, OS vectorization, and BLAS/MKL linear algebra libraries may have slightly different numerical differences between platforms. For scientific integrity, we measure all metrics using offline JSON records.
    \item \textbf{Model Complexity}: We used a lightweight multi-layer perceptron (IoMTMLP, 5,057 parameters) which can be executed at the edge. While deeper models capture the sequential motion of a client, they may require more computational overhead and overfit on smaller protocol partitions.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Limitations and Future Work}
Future work is required to use SCAFFOLD~\cite{karimireddy2020scaffold} (control variates for the drift of the client) and FedNova~\cite{wang2020tackling} (normalized averaging for heterogeneous local update) to assess the effectiveness of the second-order gradient correction when compared with a model head calibration collapse:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Advanced Drift Correctors}: Benchmarking second-order optimization methods including SCAFFOLD and FedNova to evaluate gradient correction against head personalization.
    \item \textbf{Scale-Up to Virtual Subnets}: Scaling the network partition from $K=3$ physical protocol silos to $K \ge 10$ virtual clinic subnets with varying non-IID protocol distributions.
    \item \textbf{Adaptive Local Thresholding}: Automated calibration (e.g., temperature or local Platt scaling) to adjust classification thresholds without training the model heads.
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
In this paper, we introduced FedXplain-IoMT to investigate cross-client explanation divergence and model calibration in federated intrusion detection for medical IoT. Evaluating multi-protocol telemetry from the realistic CICIoMT2024 benchmark revealed that standard FedAvg induces a catastrophic collapse in minority Bluetooth benign recall (collapsing to 0.3666--0.4238), and that FedProx proximal regularization monotonically worsens this failure down to 0.0073--0.0343. Utilizing SHAP feature attributions, we diagnosed this failure as an output calibration and threshold shift rather than internal representation divergence, as feature attribution rankings remained strongly aligned ($\rho \approx 0.98$). We proved that Personalized Federated Learning (FedPer) resolves this breakdown by retaining a shared representation trunk while decoupling the classification head, recovering 72.7\%--77.9\% of the benign recall gap and achieving 0.9241 average macro F1 while preserving cross-client explanation consistency ($\rho \ge 0.88$). Furthermore, protocol-aware Clustered FL achieved 0.9665 macro F1 and 0.9927 benign recall. Our findings establish that consistency of decision logic is measurable and that local output calibration is vital for trustworthy, privacy-preserving IoMT defense.

